Indexes 

.data structure improving ops on a DB table 
.improve query performance by organizing data efficiently 
    .allowing faster searching and retrieving of records 
    .allows location and access of rows in tables faster 
.increase speed and efficiency of DBs 
    .render a more responsive app through their strategic application 



Types 
1. Primary index 
    .automatically created when a primary key is defined on the table 
    .uniquely identifies each row in the table 
        .ensures the values are both unique and not null 
    .only one per table 


2. Unique index 
    .ensures that all values within a column are unique 
    .can be multiple per table 
    .handy in enforcing uniqueness on columns that are not the primary key



3. Full-text index 
    .used with columns storing large quantities of text 
    .facilitates very fast recovery of data based on complicated text-based searches 
    .comes with the MATCH and AGAINST clauses 
    .useful in apps like search engines 


4. Spatial index 
    .applied to geographic data types and enable the effective mgt and querying of spatial data 
    .useful in apps that involve mapping, location-based services, and geographic info systems 


5. Composite index 
    .that created on more than one column
    .performance is aided in queries that filter on more than one column, and it takes the first place in the column used in the query 



Operations:

    .CREATE INDEX idx_column_name ON table_name(column_name);
    .for composite index:
        CREATE INDEX idx_composite ON table_name(column1, column2);
    .for unique index:
        CREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_unique_column_name ON table_name(column_name);
    .for full text index:
        CREATE FULLTEXT INDEX idx_fulltext_column_name ON table_name(text_column_name);

    .showing all existing indexes:
        SHOW INDEX FROM table_name;

    .dropping an index 
        DROP INDEX idx_column_name ON table_name;

    
    .understanding how SQL is executing the indexes 
        EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM table_name WHERE column_name = 'value';

        
